# Cargo configuration template for LiteMark (MSVC toolchain).
#
# This file is the committed reference. For the MSVC toolchain the default
# rustup install is self-sufficient: rustc finds the MSVC linker (`link.exe`)
# and the Windows SDK via the standard vcvars/SDK discovery path, so **no
# machine-specific `.cargo/config.toml` is required**.
#
# Why MSVC (not GNU)?
#   LiteMark originally built with the `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` target (see ADR
#   0001) because the M0 environment had no MSVC C++ build tools. The bundled
#   self-contained MinGW linker later broke (`dlltool.exe` reported a
#   `CreateProcess` failure), blocking compilation of anything depending on
#   `windows-sys` (Tauri transitively). Meanwhile the MSVC linker became
#   available in the environment and builds + links + tests cleanly. ADR 0003
#   records this reversal: LiteMark now targets `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`,
#   Tauri's officially-preferred Windows target.
#
# Prerequisites for the MSVC target:
#   - Visual Studio 2022 Build Tools (the "Desktop development with C++"
#     workload) OR the MSVC compiler + Windows SDK standalone.
#   - Verify with:  rustc --print target-lib-dir   (succeeds when the linker
#     and SDK are discoverable).
#
# A gitignored `.cargo/config.toml` is still allowed for advanced cases (e.g.
# pinning a non-default target or custom rustflags) but it is NOT generated by
# `scripts/setup-env.{sh,ps1}` anymore. That script now only performs a sanity
# check that the MSVC toolchain can compile.
#
# If you ever need a project-local config, the minimal form is:
#
#   [build]
#   target = "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc"
